messages:
Maria L.: heads-up — enterprise customer (acme-corp) reports 400 from /v1/rerank, says scores are all zeros and we return "unexpected field: passages". they attached a sample payload. :frowning:
Sam W.: can you paste the payload they sent? are they using our new rerank API or the older /v1/rerank-beta?
Maria L.: they hit /v1/rerank (prod). payload below — note they use top-level "documents" with nested "passages" per doc, and their query key is "query" (singular). customer expects us to accept that shape because their search stack emits it.

```json
{
  "query": "How to reset password",
  "documents": [
    {
      "doc_id": "d1",
      "passages": [
        {"id": "p1", "text": "Reset via profile settings"},
        {"id": "p2", "text": "Contact admin to reset password"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "doc_id": "d2",
      "passages": [
        {"id": "p3", "text": "Use forgot password flow"}
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

Maria L.: response they get (trimmed):

```json
{ "error": "invalid_request", "message": "unexpected field 'passages' in documents; expected top-level 'candidates' array" }
```

Kevin (Support): ok — looks like the mismatch is between our documented schema and what the customer is sending. Our rerank endpoint expects `candidates` (flat list of items to score) or `documents` with flat `content` fields. Nested `passages` isn't supported currently.

Elsa - Eng: quick clarification — our internal schema historically allowed two shapes: 
- flat `candidates: [{id, text}]` (recommended for rerank),
- `documents: [{id, text}]` which we accept for legacy search outputs. We never standardized `documents[].passages` for rerank; that shape goes into the search index path which then flattens passages into candidates before scoring. So the error makes sense.

Sam W.: customer says other vendors accept per-document passages and we should too — is this a quick fix server-side?

Elsa - Eng: we could implement a compat shim: if `documents[].passages` exists, flatten to candidates by concatenating passage text or by creating candidate entries per passage with ids like `d1:p1`. Risk: changes tokenization and scoring expectations. Also needs to preserve candidate->document mapping for downstream metrics. Low to medium risk, but needs tests.

Kevin (Support): short term we can give a repro and a migrator snippet for the customer. here's a suggested transformation they can run client-side:

```js
// pseudo-transform
const payload = clientPayload;
const candidates = [];
for (const doc of (payload.documents || [])) {
  if (doc.passages) {
    for (const p of doc.passages) {
      candidates.push({ id: `${doc.doc_id}:${p.id}`, text: p.text });
    }
  } else if (doc.text) {
    candidates.push({ id: doc.doc_id, text: doc.text });
  }
}
const body = { query: payload.query, candidates };
```

Maria L.: I'll send that to the customer and propose the client-side workaround. also asking whether we should add a note in docs and maybe a compatibility flag on the API.

Elsa - Eng: docs update = yes. compatibility flag = possible but needs PM signoff. if we add server-side flattening it should be opt-in behind `accept_passages=true` for a release cycle, then consider default later.

Sam W.: can you add a quick doc snippet and an example curl we can paste into the ticket? i'll attach repro steps.

Elsa - Eng: sample curl (works for their data after transform):

```bash
curl -s -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/rerank \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $REDWOOD_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"query":"How to reset password","candidates":[{"id":"d1:p1","text":"Reset via profile settings"},{"id":"d1:p2","text":"Contact admin to reset password"},{"id":"d2:p3","text":"Use forgot password flow"}] }'
```

Kevin (Support): i'll paste the transformed payload and the curl into the support ticket and mark customer workaround as provided. Ticket: #CS-4721.

Maria L.: customer also observed weird scores (all zeros) when they tried another shape — could that be an unrelated bug? they included the raw response from our service logs: `scores: [0,0,0]` after a 200. looks like model returns 0 prob on that input.

Elsa - Eng: if they get 200 with zeros, that's likely they passed an empty `candidates` array or our tokenizer produced empty strings due to unexpected encoding. please ask for base64 or unicode escapes. Also check if their `query` key was `queries` (plural) which maps to rerank-batch flow and expects different shapes; passing singular `query` into batch path could confuse the scorer and leave defaults.

Sam W.: action items:
- Maria: send workaround + curl + request for full failing payloads and raw service logs (CS-4721)
- Kevin: add docs PR for `documents[].passages` note + caveat about flattening and mapping (assign to docs-team)
- Elsa: estimate for compat shim behind flag (assign to eng-runtime backlog)

Maria L.: i'll message the customer now and update the ticket. tagging @Elsa-Eng if they want to follow the ticket. :thumbsup:

deploy-bot: incident follow-up reminder: please ensure post-mortem if this becomes an outage (none detected).

Elsa - Eng: thanks — let's keep the thread updated. cc @docs-team when PR is ready.

Maria L.: thanks everyone, closing this slack thread and moving convo to ticket until we have docs PR.
